h services: a cross sectional survey</article-title><source>BMJ</source><year>1998</year><volume>316</volume><fpage>524</fpage><lpage>27</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9501716</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Turnbull</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Martin</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Lattimer</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Pope</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Culliford</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Does distance matter? Geographical variation in GP out-of-hours service use: An observational study</article-title><source>Br J Gen Pract</source><year>2008</year><volume>58</volume><fpage>471</fpage><lpage>77</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18611312</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.3399/bjgp08X319431</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>O'Reilly</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Stevensen</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>McCay</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Jamison</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>General practice out-of-hours services, variation in use and equality in access to a doctor: a cross -sectional study</article-title><source>Br J Gen Pract</source><year>2001</year><volume>51</volume><fpage>625</fpage><lpage>29</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11510390</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Livingstone</surname><given-names>AE</given-names></name><name><surname>Jewell</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name><name><surname>Robson</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Twenty four hour care in inner cities; Two years' out of hours workload in east London general practice</article-title><source>Br Med J</source><year>1989</year><volume>299</volume><fpage>368</fpage><lpage>70</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2506972</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1136/bmj.299.6695.368</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Leibowitz</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Day</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Dunt</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A systematic review of the effect of different models of after-hours primary medical care services on clinical outcome, medical workload, and patient and GP satisfaction</article-title><source>Fam Pract</source><year>2003</year><volume>20</volume><fpage>311</fpage><lpage>17</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12738701</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/fampra/cmg313</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Dunt</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Wilson</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Day</surname><given-names>SE</given-names></name><name><surname>Kelaher</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Gurrin</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Impact of telephone triage on emergency after hours GP Medicare usage: A time-series analysis</article-title><source>Aust New Zealand Health Policy</source><year>2007</year><volume>4</volume><fpage>21</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17927836</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/1743-8462-4-21</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>